WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.772
Partitioning social data is difficult because
different readers want the same post near

00:00:04.772 --> 00:00:09.599
different places. A viewer's user row and
follow-list entries can share a shard, but the

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:13.164
authors they follow may be distributed across
the entire service.

00:00:13.164 --> 00:00:18.111
Placing one author's posts beside one follower
helps that follower without necessarily

00:00:18.111 --> 00:00:23.289
helping the others. A popular author can have
followers on every shard. This is a conflict

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:27.719
in the access pattern, not a failure to invent a
clever enough hash function.

00:00:27.719 --> 00:00:32.812
The diagram should place followers on two shards
and connect both to one author, showing why

00:00:32.812 --> 00:00:36.299
a single physical home cannot be local to every
reader at once.

00:00:36.299 --> 00:00:41.273
Represent the follow structure as a graph. Users
are nodes and follow relationships are

00:00:41.273 --> 00:00:46.475
edges. A balanced partitioning tries to
distribute load while keeping frequently used
edges

00:00:46.475 --> 00:00:51.621
within partitions. Communities can improve
locality, but cross-community relationships and

00:00:51.621 --> 00:00:53.965
high-degree users still create crossings.

00:00:53.965 --> 00:00:58.920
A perfectly balanced count of users can also
conceal large differences in query rate and

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:04.101
audience size. Weight the graph by the
operations that actually traverse it, not just
by the

00:01:04.101 --> 00:01:09.113
existence of a relationship. Moving an account
between partitions can change locality for

00:01:09.113 --> 00:01:12.379
several neighbors and requires routing and
migration work.

00:01:12.465 --> 00:01:17.290
The useful question is how much communication a
placement avoids for the measured workload,

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:19.942
rather than whether it makes an appealing
picture.

00:01:19.965 --> 00:01:24.964
A single publication policy becomes inefficient
when follower counts vary by orders of

00:01:24.964 --> 00:01:30.254
magnitude. An ordinary author with a few hundred
followers may be inexpensive to fan out. A

00:01:30.254 --> 00:01:34.963
highly followed author can create millions of
feed updates for one post, delaying

00:01:34.963 --> 00:01:39.962
propagation and consuming resources even for
followers who never open the application.

00:01:39.965 --> 00:01:44.883
A queue keeps the publisher from waiting for all
those updates, but it does not make them

00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:49.745
disappear. The hybrid idea is to keep
materialized feeds for ordinary publications
while

00:01:49.745 --> 00:01:54.608
storing very high-fan-out authors' posts in a
separate path that can be consulted when a

00:01:54.608 --> 00:01:55.879
reader requests a feed.

00:01:55.965 --> 00:02:01.222
A feed request now has two inputs. Fetch the
reader's prepared ordinary-author entries, then

00:02:01.222 --> 00:02:06.308
fetch recent posts from the exceptional authors
that reader follows, and merge them using

00:02:06.308 --> 00:02:11.109
the feed's ordering rules. This adds bounded
read-time work in exchange for avoiding

00:02:11.109 --> 00:02:13.166
enormous publication-time expansion.

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:18.349
The exceptional-post store can have read
replicas or caches because relatively few
authors

00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:23.302
generate its writes while many readers retrieve
them. The important asymmetry is write

00:02:23.302 --> 00:02:28.543
amplification per publication, not a claim that
famous users intrinsically perform more API

00:02:28.543 --> 00:02:29.579
writes than reads.

00:02:29.666 --> 00:02:34.539
Draw both sources converging at the merge step
so students can see precisely which work was

00:02:34.539 --> 00:02:36.520
retained and which work was deferred.
